before:
```react
class SmallBoundary extends React.Component {
  constructor(props) { super(props); this.state = {failed:false}; }
  static getDerivedStateFromError() { return {failed:true}; }
  render() { return this.state.failed ? <p>Try again later</p> : this.props.children; }
}
```

To exercise the boundary, render it around a child that fails:
```react
function BrokenPanel() { throw new Error("Demo failure"); }
function App() {
  return <SmallBoundary><BrokenPanel /></SmallBoundary>;
}
```
With SmallBoundary defined above, rendering App shows Try again later. React calls the static error method with the thrown error; this example does not use that parameter. The method returns a state update. React then calls render, which takes no explicit arguments and returns fallback UI. The framework invokes these methods; do not call them yourself to simulate handling.

new Error("Demo failure") constructs an Error object with that message. throw interrupts the child’s render by throwing that object. The nearest suitable boundary can then choose its fallback; Error construction alone, without throw, would not signal a render failure.

after:
```react
class SmallBoundary extends React.Component {
  constructor(props) {
    super(props);
    this.state = { failed: false };
  }
  static getDerivedStateFromError() {
    return { failed: true };
  }
  render() {
    return this.state.failed
      ? <p>Try again later</p>
      : this.props.children;
  }
}
function BrokenPanel() {
  throw new Error("Demo failure");
}
function App() {
  return <SmallBoundary><BrokenPanel /></SmallBoundary>;
}
```
BrokenPanel throws an error on purpose. The boundary shows `Try again later` instead. Creating an Error object alone would not throw it.
